>He was already satiated, but wraith corpses were so tasty "you can't eat just one!"  He
>cast remove sickness once to make room in his belly, then ate the corpse.  All of a
>sudden he felt sensitive. Hooray! (Side note: Did casting a spell help me avoid choking?)

Aside from making you wait one extra turn to digest food, no, I don't
believe casting that spell helped you. The way choking on food works is
if you have a current nutrition value of 2000 or greater eating
*anything* will cause you to die from choking, unless wearing an amulet
of magical breathing. Being satiated means that your nutrition is
greater than 1000, so that's as much help you get there.

Wraith corpses have a nutrition value of 0, so as long as you aren't
already above 2000 nutritional value there shouldn't be any risk from
eating them (unless the meat is tainted). Just for something to compare
those nutrition values to here are the nutrition values for a few
selected monsters:

Dragons: 1500
Giants: 750
Most medium sized creatures: 300-500
Huge chunk of meat: 2000
Food ration: 800
K-ration: 400

You will get a message that asks you if you want to stop eating if you
are going from say, 1000 nutrition and consuming a giant hunk of meat.
This will take you to slightly over 2000 nutrition if you choose to
stop eating when it prompts you. If you then decide to finish your meal
with a nice fortune cookie for dessert, or a wraith corpse, you will
die with no option to stop eating.

Ways to decrease your nutrition if there's a wraith corpse sitting
there and you just "finally finished" a meal.

Don't wear a ring of slow digestion. Wear a ring of regen on one hand,
and a ring of hunger on the other hand. Have an encumbrance of
stressed, strained or overtaxed, wear an amulet, and be carrying the
Amulet of Yendor.

If you meet all of these conditions you should consume 64 nutrition
every 20 turns. I'm not sure how long it takes for a wraith corpse to
rot away, but if you just finished eating a big meal you need to wait
at least some turns for your nutrition to fall to a safe value before
consumption.


Sorry to get so extensive here, but I didn't know the exact answer to
your question so I researched it, and those were all of my findings.

